{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The problem explicitly asks 'which of the two properties' (idempotency or safety) an append-only log requires of its writers, and instructs drawing on 'published definitions.' SRC_001 refuses to pick either, instead coining 'monotonic-append-safety' with no published basis. More damningly, its own first countercondition concedes the entire claim collapses if this third property is a special case of generic safety — and it is: preserving 'immutability, monotonic growth, tail-ordering' is exactly safety (preserving specific structural invariants of the system). SRC_002's own definition of safety as 'preserving the invariants of a specific system' subsumes this. The target thus fails to answer the asked question and its novel distinction is self-undermining.","counterexample":[{"instance":"An operation that preserves the invariants {immutability, monotonic growth, tail-ordering} of an append-only log is safe by definition — it preserves the system's specific invariants. Calling it a 'third property' adds nothing.","property":"safety"}],"target":"15b4cc9e8b595a7b01f8b15b5d76b1352da94c375dafd356b3e6bd7401c22f2b"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 argues the log requires idempotency, but its own mechanism describes deduplication — 'detect that an event was already appended and refuse the duplicate' — which is a safety-preserving invariant check, not algebraic idempotency. A raw append is inherently non-idempotent: append(append(L, x), x) ≠ append(L, x) because it yields two entries. Wrapping append with an event-ID existence check enforces the invariant 'each event appears at most once,' which by SRC_002's own definition ('preserves the invariants of a specific system') is safety, not idempotency. The target thus argues for idempotency while its own causal mechanism demonstrates safety. Separately, the resource-exhaustion argument addresses liveness/operational degradation, not the deterministic-replay correctness the problem centers on ('Deterministic replay depends on retried operations being safe to repeat').","counterexample":[{"instance":"Writer W checks 'event_id in log?' before appending. This preserves the invariant {no duplicate events}, which is safety. The bare append op f(L,x)=L++[x] is non-idempotent: f(f(L,x),x)=L++[x,x]≠L++[x]=f(L,x). So the writer is safe, not idempotent.","property":"idempotency_vs_safety"}],"target":"e62bf3631e0980734777c2e1d8e03b91b1191a03cff586a0a729557fbbd9caa1"}]}